s attacks on civilian  objects so the attack on the hospital  there's a strong strong case to answer  um that that is um a  a war crime a breach  of the uh geneva conventions which  protect um civilians  um in the time of war talk to me about  how evidence is gathered we're looking  at a video here of what happened at that  hospital in maripo um but what would the  international criminal court for example  be looking at how is a case put together  well  um as the uh prosecutor at the icc is  seeing to doing he's sending a team of  investigators so you're looking at me  you're looking at  obviously what's happened on the ground  you've been looking at video evidence  and then ultimately you'll also be  relying upon the the ev the evidence of  military analysts such as the air com  commodore you had on previously looking  at analyzing all that and that's  particularly important for offences such  as genocide  and and crimes against uh humanity  because you're looking at  murder on a widespread scale as part of  an overall plan  probably um  grave breaches  of the geneva convention  are war crimes which are most  straightforward um to prove  um  but it's  it's a case of putting the pieces  together  as as in any other cry isn't any a crime  it's gathering all the pieces of  evidence together  um you mentioned before that those who  can be liable  for war crimes go from the bottom to the  top so we're talking about foot soldiers  general all the way up to vladimir putin  himself and i was wondering if you could  explain something to me that has been  talked about this week the labour leader  sikhistan has brought it up the mp tom  tuggenheart and the foreign secretary  liz trust talked about it and this is  the crime of aggression  under which political leaders and  military chiefs can be held individually  responsible for an invasion that is  being talked about as being leveled of  vladimir putin but there is a difficulty  in holding him to account for that can  you explain it to us  well they're they're looking they're  looking at the nature of the aggression  which  nature of the war which russia has  unleashed against ukraine but even if  you were trying um vladimir putin  for crimes at the international criminal  court for for war crimes you're going to  have  there is a problem  in that of course he's in russia  and he's not going to voluntarily  surrender  so getting him  to stand trial  is going to be very very difficult and  you you and your viewers remember  that you know the war crimes  prosecutions in bosnia herzegovina and  how long it took  to get uh radhavan kharagic the leader  of the republic of sarpska in bosnia  herzegovina and the leader of the  bosnian serbs to get him to stand trial  obviously he went underground  so  it's not going to be easy to put anybody  at the top level  on trial maybe easier at the bottom  level but the other thing to remember is  that this is not going to happen  soon it's going to be you know  if any there is any prosecution  uh unfortunately it's going to be a long  time in the future  mark guthrie human rights lawyer we  really appreciate you uh talking us  through all of that thank you for your  time this afternoon here on sky news  thank you
 

